-- title:
--   Section of a smooth relative curve: principal, transverse section ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative local ring, let $X$ be a scheme, and let $c \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ be a morphism that is smooth of relative dimension $1$. Let $\sigma \colon \operatorname{Spec} A \to X$ be a section of $c$, i.e. $\sigma$ followed by $c$ is the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Write $s = \sigma(\mathfrak{m}_A)$ for the image under $\sigma$ of the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and let $S = \mathcal{O}_{X,s}$ be the stalk of $X$ there, a local ring. The assertion is that there exists $t \in S$ such that, first, the kernel of the ring map on stalks induced by $\sigma$ at the closed point of $\operatorname{Spec} A$, namely $S \to \mathcal{O}_{\operatorname{Spec} A, \mathfrak{m}_A}$, is the principal ideal $(t)$; and second, the maximal ideal of $S$ is the sum of $(t)$ and the ideal of $S$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$ under the structural ring homomorphism $A \cong \Gamma(\operatorname{Spec} A, \mathcal{O}) \to \Gamma(X, \mathcal{O}_X) \to S$ obtained from the inverse of the global-sections isomorphism for $\operatorname{Spec} A$, the map $c$ on global sections, and the germ map at $s$ from the top open set.
--
--   This is the local statement that a section of a smooth relative curve over a local base is cut out at its closed point by a single equation, and that this equation restricts to a uniformiser on the closed fibre, i.e. the section meets the closed fibre transversally; no noetherian hypothesis is imposed on $A$, the intended bases including non-discrete valuation rings. It is deduced from the retraction lemma for the stalk of such a section, and is used to show that the relevant local rings on the closed fibre are one-dimensional discrete valuation rings and to compute orders of vanishing of sections on models of modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_exists_ker_stalkMap_eq_span_and_maximalIdeal_eq_of_section_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one.lean

